//@ unimplemented "All four imports resolve and the whole file parses, `pub implements c_printf = printf;` on 15 included. The fourth import is a repair, 2026-08-27, under the owner's ruling that a sample whose own logic is wrong may be repaired rather than booked: line 8's blank now carries `import { getkeyid, keyidof } from enums::std;`, because 57, 65 and 71 called both names and this file imported neither. Nothing here is invented -- stdlib/typing.fin:4 writes that line character for character, and tests/samples/stdlib/enums.fin:17 declares `pub fun getkeyid(value: EnumType) <int>` and :22 `pub fun keyidof(enum_member: $enum_member) <int>`, both `pub`, both inside `namespace std`, both in module `enums`, a sibling entry in this directory. Six diagnostics went and none arrived. The question this repair used to ride on is now ruled on, and the ruling confirms the repair rather than excusing it. What was called \"the prelude question\" was two questions. `printf` is the invention half: a declaration marked `#[global]` will be visible to every file in the session with no import, the attribute is usable only inside the `std` namespace, and `printf` alone is to carry it. `getkeyid` and `keyidof` are the ambience half -- they are declared `pub` in a sibling module and are exactly the \"everything else\" that stays non-global -- and the owner has ruled that ambience does not exist, so a file that uses them owes an import. This import is that debt paid, not a workaround for an open question. Two loops are repaired with it. 114 read `for (i: int = 0; i >= _temp.length; i++)` and 126 `i >= self.stream_length`; both are initialised to 0 and incremented, so `>=` made each body unreachable, and each body copies a buffer forward. The extent is measured rather than taken on trust: three dead loops exist in the whole tree and no more -- these two and const.fin:67 -- against seven `i <` loops written in the same slot (arrays.fin:17, :18, loops.fin:14, lib/std/collection.fin:78, lib/std/hashmap.fin:52, lib/std/stdio.fin:139, :151), and the last two of those are this file's own two methods as the derived library writes them. lib/std/stdio.fin:55-57 names the defect and is loose about where it lives: it says the draft is wrong \"in three places and stdlib/collection.fin repeats it\", but only two of the three are in this draft, the third is const.fin:67, and stdlib/collection.fin:55 reads `i <= self.filled` -- an off-by-one, not a dead loop. Both edits are one operator each and the import replaced a blank line, so no line number moved: this file is cited by line forty-one times across src/, tests/ and docs/, at 1, 11, 15, 23, 31, 33, 35, 36, 42, 44, 47, 48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 54, 55, 56, 58, 60, 63, 65, 83, 87, 88, 93, 97, 98, 107, 108, 109, 110, 112, 114, 124, 130, 153, 154, 156 and 160, and an inserted line would have staled all of them at once. Thirteen are left. That count is not twenty-three less ten: the twenty-three this note used to open with was itself stale, and nineteen is what the file measured immediately before this repair. `format!` on 36 is a macro this file never declares and no file in the corpus or lib/std defines, and `Strict` on 49 and `PathLike` on 144 are types declared in no corpus file either -- none of the three is the prelude question in either half, because ambience cannot make visible a name that is nowhere written, and none may be invented. Line 65 read `geykeyid` and is repaired to `getkeyid`: that is the spelling lines 57 and 71 use, the spelling tests/samples/stdlib/enums.fin:17 and lib/std/enums.fin:24 declare, and the spelling stdlib/typing.fin:4 imports, while `geykeyid` was declared nowhere. It used to swap one `Undefined function or type` for another; with the import above it resolves outright. Line 156 read `OK(...)` and is repaired to `Ok(...)`: line 51 of this file declares the member as `pub Ok <T>`, 57 and 71 name it `Ok`, and the sibling branch on 154 names `Err` exactly as line 50 declares it. What that uncovered is `Invalid cast from 'string' to '[char]'` on the same line -- whether a string literal casts to a `[char]` is an owner ruling this file is the corpus's only site for, and until it is made the diagnostic is correct. `Type 'X' does not have methods` on 24 and 29 is a generic bound reached through an alias to `any`, which loses the interface's methods (KnownDefect_TypeAliases.GenericArgumentsOnANonGenericAliasAreDiscarded). The four `Struct 'IOResult' has no member '0'` are gone: positional payload access reads slot N of whichever member the value holds, and this file is what rules out reading the position as an index into a flattening of the enum -- `IOResult` declares `Err` first and `Ok` second, and 58 still reads the `Ok` payload as `.0` where typing.fin reads it as `.0` with the members in the other order. `Err <IOError>` and `Ok <T>` disagree at position 0, so the slot is an `any`, which the `<T>` return on 58 accepts and which `blame` on 60 and 66 raises rather than compares against bool (Soundness_PositionalMembers, Soundness_Blame). `Cannot assign to immutable variable 'nbytes'` on 110 is gone: a parameter is mutable unless it is written `const`, and this file is one of the two sites that need it -- `nbytes: ulong = -1` is a sentinel the body replaces with `self.stream_length`, the same shape as error.fin:13 (Soundness_ParameterMutability). The five between 110 and 135 are one rule: `size` and `nbytes` are `ulong` and every buffer length and index is an `int`, and no conversion between integer types exists -- the owner ruled on 2026-08-27 that integer conversion is implicit widening wherever no value is lost, and cited these very sites as the evidence -- eleven of them, `ulong` lengths against `int` indices with no cast written anywhere, which is not how a language that wanted explicit conversion would read. So the cascade is the compiler's to implement and not this sample's to fix; the diagnostics stand until it is. The two `expected '[char]', got '&[char; fixed]'` on 120 and 129 are gone: a `new [char, n]` is a `[char]`, not a pointer to a fixed-size one, and this file is half the evidence -- `_temp.length` on 130 reads a length off the allocation, which a pointer does not have (Soundness_HeapArrays). The `expected 'IOError', got 'string'` on 154 is correct as the sample is written: `Err` carries an `IOError` and a string literal is handed to it. What it took to get there is that the construction is now typed as the instantiation -- `return Err(...)` inside `<IOResult<Stream>>` reads the enclosing function's declared return type for what no argument mentions, which is the only place this call has to say which `IOResult` it builds (Soundness_EnumInference.AReturnSeedsFromTheEnclosingFunction). `File does not implement FileIO` on 149 is correct as the sample is written -- File declares no `get_file_info`. `Stream does not implement IStream` was here too and is gone: IStream requires a constructor as `Self(data: [char])`, Stream writes `Stream(data: [char])`, and the two are matched on their parameters now (Soundness_InterfaceConstructors)"
#[stdimport] %{
import { Any } from types::std;
import { Error } from error::std;
import { IResult } from typing::std;
}%
import { getkeyid, keyidof } from enums::std;
namespace std {

#[llvm_name="c_printf"]
@define printf(fmt: string, ...) <noret>;

#[export]
pub implements c_printf = printf; // Symbol Resolution

#[export]
pub interface Printable {
   pub fun format_str() <string>;
}

#[export]
pub fun print<X: Any<Printable>>(object: X) <noret> {
   c_printf(object.format_str());
}

#[export]
pub fun println<X: Any<Printable>>(object: X) <noret> {
    c_printf("%s\n",object.format_str());
  }

#[llvm_name="printf"]
#[overwrite(printf)] // this tells the compiler we are overwriting printf and it will ignore the current definition of it and doesn't raise an error
#[export]
pub fun printf<X: Any<Printable>>(fmt: string, ...objects: [X]) <noret> {
    let formatted_str <string> = format!(fmt, ...objects); // Using format macro
    c_printf(formatted_str);
  }


// forward declaration
#[future] // compiler will look into the future if necessary to avoid complications
struct Stream;

#[export]
struct IOError: <Error> {}

#[export]
pub enum IOResult<T: Strict<Stream>> { // Strict is a classifier making the generic strictly attached to the type provided to it in this case `Stream`
    pub Err <IOError>,
    pub Ok <T>,
  }

IOResult<T> implements <IResult> {
    pub:
      fun unwrap(enum_ : IOResult<T>) <T> {
        if (getkeyid(enum_) == keyidof(Ok)) {
            return enum_.0;
          } else {
              blame enum_.0;
            }
      }

      fun expect(enum_ : IOResult<T>) <noret> {
        if (getkeyid(enum_) == keyidof(Err)){
            blame enum_.0;
          }
      }

      fun select(enum_ : IOResult<T>, callback : fn(T) -> void) <noret> {
        if (getkeyid(enum_) == keyidof(Ok)) {
            callback(enum_.0);
          }
      }
  }


#[export]
pub interface IStream {
  priv:
      stream_length <ulong>;
      pointer <ulong>;
      stream <[char]>;

  pub:
    fun seek(offset: ulong) <bool>;
    fun read(nbytes: ulong = -1) <[char]>;
    fun expand(nbytes: ulong) <noret>;

  Self(data: [char]); // constructor
  }

#[export]
struct Stream : <IStream> {
    priv:
      stream_length <ulong>,
      pointer <ulong> = 0, // default=0
      stream <[char]>,

    pub:
      fun seek(offset: ulong) <bool> {
          if (offset > self.stream_length) {
            return false;
          } else {
            self.pointer = offset;
            return true;
            }
        }
      fun read(nbytes: ulong = -1) <[char]> {
          if (nbytes > self.stream_length || nbytes == -1) nbytes = self.stream_length;

          let _temp <auto> = new [char, nbytes - self.pointer];

          for (i: int = 0; i < _temp.length; i++) {
            _temp[i] = self.stream[i+self.pointer];
          }

          self.pointer += nbytes;

          return _temp;
        }

      fun expand(nbytes: ulong) <noret> {
          let _temp <auto> = new [char, nbytes + self.stream_length];

          for (i: int = 0; i < self.stream_length; i++){
              _temp[i] = self.stream[i];
            }
          self.stream = _temp;
          self.stream_length = _temp.length;
        }

      Stream(data: [char]) {
          self.stream = data;
          self.stream_length = data.length;
        }

      //TODO: ... (write, append, ...)
  }


#[export]
pub interface FileIO {
    pub fun get_file_info(path: PathLike) <IOResult>;
    pub fun open(path: string) <IOResult<Stream>>;
    pub fun exists(path: string) <bool>;
  }

#[export]
pub struct File: <FileIO> {
    pub:
      static fun open(path: string) <IOResult<Stream>> {
        if (!File::exists(path)){
            return Err("File don't exists");
          } else {
              return Ok(Stream(cast<[char]>("SomeData for testing")));
            }
      }
      static fun exists(path: string) <bool> {
          return path.length == 10 : true ? false; // for testing
        }
  }
}
